JD — CURRENT ACTIVE DEFINITIONS (SYSTEM REGISTRY)

Core Systems

YGGDRASIL → Global hierarchical ontology backbone; conceptual root structure for all subsystems.

JFS (Jarvis File System) → Structural kernel defining repository, folder, module organization and system layout rules.

JSL (Jarvis Structure Language) → Governance rules for logs, patches, file structure, and hierarchy enforcement.

JNL (Jarvis Navigation Language) → Global identity and routing system assigning unique IDs for all entities, logs, systems, and references.

JD (Jarvis Dictionary) → Semantic registry mapping JNL-linked identifiers to meanings, definitions, and system concepts.

JMS (Jarvis Mirror System) → Synchronization and mirroring layer that reduces redundancy by reflecting system state across repositories.



---

Execution + Control Systems

JARVIS → Execution and validation authority layer responsible for enforcing JFS/JSL compliance and committing canonical system state across external systems (GitHub, Supabase, MCP tools).

LIMINAL → Runtime cognitive mode controller managing reasoning states (STANDARD, AYRE, JARVIS, HYBRID) and re-anchoring system coherence during drift.

JPT (Jarvis Protocol Transform) → Output formatting and reasoning constraint layer enforcing structured response behavior and mode-based cognition formatting.



---

Memory + Entropy Systems

CHAOS → Append-only entropy ingestion layer storing raw outputs, system logs, experiments, and full system snapshots for replay and recovery.

FATES → High-compression predictive semantic layer that transforms accumulated system state into dense forecasting packets (experimental/high-risk abstraction layer).

GRAVEYARD → Ultra-compressed archival encoding layer for long-term minimized representation of historical system state.

JORMUNGANDR → Experimental machine-language compression system for high-density symbolic encoding of system data.



---

Structural / Conceptual Layers

GOD SYSTEMS → Role-based functional decomposition framework (e.g., AEGIS, ERIS, ODIN) used to separate governance, entropy, routing, and enforcement functions.



---

System Relationships (Implicit JD Knowledge)

JNL provides identity keys for all JD entries

JD maps JNL → semantic meaning

JFS defines where JD entries conceptually belong in structure

CHAOS stores raw versions of JD state evolution

JARVIS validates and commits JD updates

LIMINAL controls when JD context is actively enforced in reasoning

JMS ensures JD consistency across mirrored repositories



